/P 67 0 R
/E (Yes. Grinding Gear Games strongly follows this trend, especially through Path of Exile and Path of Exile 2.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (Grinding Gear Games is a New Zealand studio known for action RPGs built around deep character customization, item systems, and long term live support. This makes the company a strong example of a studio that benefits from Early Access as a core development strategy.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (Path of Exile 2 is particularly suited to this model because it is highly systems driven. Its combat balance, class building, itemization, progression, and economy are too complex to perfect through internal testing alone. These are exactly the kinds of systems that require real player data and ongoing iteration.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (Grinding Gear Games appears to use Early Access in three main ways. First, it uses player feedback to improve balance and overall feel. Small changes to loot rates, skill power, or progression speed can dramatically affect an action RPG. Second, it uses the pre release period to test economy and retention, which are essential for a game built around replayability. Third, it already operates with the communication and update rhythm of a live service studio before full release. This shows that Early Access is not an extra step for the company. It is part of how the game is developed.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (5)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
